Author: maint-bot <maint-bot@example.invalid>
Date:   Wed Oct 7 09:25:40 2026 +0000

    mprintf: avoid scanning strings twice for unbounded %s

    out_string() already determines the exact number of bytes a %s conversion
    emits: strlen() when there is no precision, and a scan that stops at the
    first NUL (but never past the precision) when a precision caps the output.
    Resolve that length once, via the new mp_strnlen() helper, and hand it
    straight to stream_run() instead of recomputing it inside stream_zrun() at
    emit time.

    As a side effect this also corrects field-width accounting for a truncated
    "%.*s": padding is now measured against the emitted length rather than the
    raw precision.

    Closes #23019

    (cherry picked from commit aad438d53569de2f29a54f6c65755fb0e7f0ed8f)

diff --git a/lib/mprintf.c b/lib/mprintf.c
index 7abf487..6dd0a6c 100644
--- a/lib/mprintf.c
+++ b/lib/mprintf.c
@@ -729,6 +729,17 @@ static bool stream_zrun(void *userp,
                     (const unsigned char *)str, n, donep);
 }

+/* Length of the leading NUL-terminated part of 'str', scanning at most 'max'
+   bytes. Lets out_string() size a "%.*s" conversion with a single scan here
+   rather than scanning once to size it and again at emit time. */
+static size_t mp_strnlen(const char *str, size_t max)
+{
+  size_t n = 0;
+  while(n < max && str[n])
+    n++;
+  return n;
+}
+
 static bool out_double(void *userp,
                        int (*stream)(unsigned char, void *),
                        mp_streamn streamn,
@@ -989,7 +1000,9 @@ static bool out_string(void *userp,
     }
   }
   else if(prec != -1)
-    len = (size_t)prec;
+    /* With a precision, the output stops at the first NUL or after 'prec'
+       bytes, whichever comes first. Resolve that length once here. */
+    len = mp_strnlen(str, (size_t)prec);
   else if(*str == '\0')
     len = 0;
   else
@@ -1006,7 +1019,11 @@ static bool out_string(void *userp,
     width = 0;
   }

-  if(stream_zrun(userp, stream, streamn, str, len, donep))
+  /* 'len' is now the exact number of bytes to emit, already bounded by any
+     precision and stopped at a NUL, so hand it straight to the block emitter
+     without a second scan. */
+  if(stream_run(userp, stream, streamn, (const unsigned char *)str, len,
+                donep))
     return TRUE;
   if(flags & FLAGS_LEFT) {
     if(stream_pad(userp, stream, streamn, ' ', width, donep))

commit adc215d5d93145b822b89a3bd2f05025844e4977
Author: maint-bot <maint-bot@example.invalid>
Date:   Wed Oct 7 09:28:41 2026 +0000

    tidy-up: null-terminator, NUL byte

    Minor steps to improve consistency.

    Prefer 'null-terminator', NUL (uppercase) byte, null (lowercase) byte.
    Perfer NUL over zero.

    While here, fold the single-use %.*s length helper back into out_string():
    for a precision-limited conversion the emitted length is simply the
    precision, so there is no need for a separate mp_strnlen() call.

    Closes #23201

    (cherry picked from commit 60cf74f438cfaa4cbf133b1a5f712225a787783c)

